+# Travel Safety Advisory
+
+Resolves #772 — Intelligent Travel Risk Assessment and Safety Advisory
+System.
+
+A fully client-side, rule-based safety layer: no backend, no ML model, no
+API keys. For any of the ~48 destinations already in `trip-data.js`, or
+for every stop in a saved Trip Planner itinerary, it produces a 0-100
+safety score, a list of travel advisories, a personalized checklist, live
+nearby hospitals/police stations, and India's national emergency contact
+directory.
+
+## Files added
+
+| File | Purpose |
+| ---- | ------- |
+| `js-modules/safety-data.js` | Static reference data: nationwide emergency contact numbers, curated hazard tables (high-altitude, cyclone belt + season, monsoon landslide zones, BIS seismic zones, desert heat season, wildlife-viewing spots), and the base safety-score-by-category heuristic. |
+| `js-modules/travel-safety-core.js` | Pure scoring/advisory/checklist/itinerary-alert logic. No DOM, no fetch — unit-testable in isolation. |
+| `js-modules/travel-safety-service.js` | Live-data fetch/cache layer: recent nearby earthquakes (USGS) and nearby hospitals/police (OpenStreetMap Overpass API). Reuses `weather-service.js` for the weather risk input rather than duplicating it. |
+| `js-modules/travel-safety-ui.js` | DOM layer for the page (`initTravelSafetyPage`): destination picker, single-destination safety check, saved-itinerary safety check, checklist persistence. |
+| `frontend/travel-safety/travel-safety.html` | The page shell (header/nav/footer match the rest of the site) plus the destination picker, results panel, itinerary-alerts panel, and emergency-contacts panel. |
+| `frontend/travel-safety/travel-safety.css` | Dedicated stylesheet, reusing the site's existing design tokens (`--saffron`, `--glass-bg`, `--glass-border`). |
+| `tests/unit/travel-safety-core.test.js` | Unit tests for the scoring, advisory, checklist, and itinerary-alert logic. |
+| `js-modules/router-init.js` (modified) | New `travel-safety.html` entry in `ROUTE_INIT_MAP`, registering the page's script dependencies and `initTravelSafetyPage` init function. |
+| Trip Planner / Budget Planner / 3D Map / Travel Timeline pages (modified) | Added a "🛡️ Travel Safety" nav link alongside the existing "🧳 Trip Planner" link so the feature is discoverable from the pages travelers already use to plan a trip. |
+
+## How it fits into the existing architecture
+
+Route → script wiring goes through the declarative `ROUTE_INIT_MAP` in
+`router-init.js` (the same mechanism `trip-planner.html` uses): when the
+SPA router navigates to `travel-safety.html`, it lazy-loads
+`trip-data.js`, `trip-planner.js` (for the saved-trips list),
+`safety-data.js`, `weather-core.js` + `weather-service.js` (reused, not
+duplicated), then `travel-safety-core.js` → `travel-safety-service.js` →
+`travel-safety-ui.js`, and finally calls `initTravelSafetyPage()`.
+
+The module split mirrors the Weather-Aware Itinerary feature
+(`weather-core.js` / `weather-service.js` / `weather-ui.js`, issue #286):
+
+- **`travel-safety-core.js`** — pure functions only. Given a destination
+  and an already-resolved context (weather suitability, recent
+  earthquakes), it returns a score, advisories, and a checklist. No
+  network access, so it's fully unit-testable.
+- **`travel-safety-service.js`** — the only file that touches `fetch`/
+  `localStorage`. Isolated so scoring logic doesn't need a mocked network
+  to test.
+- **`travel-safety-ui.js`** — the only file that touches the DOM.
+
+## Data sources
+
+| Signal | Source | Notes |
+| ------ | ------ | ----- |
+| Weather risk | Open-Meteo (via the existing `weather-service.js` + `weather-core.js`) | Not duplicated — the safety score reuses `WeatherCore.computeDaySuitability()`'s output as one input. |
+| Recent earthquakes | [USGS Earthquake Hazards Program FDSN event API](https://earthquake.usgs.gov/fdsnws/event/1/) | Free, no API key. Queried for magnitude ≥ 3.5 within 300 km of the destination in the last 30 days. |
+| Nearby hospitals & police | [OpenStreetMap Overpass API](https://overpass-api.de/api/interpreter) | Free, no API key — the same open geodata source the route planner already trusts (see `docs/ROUTE_PLANNER.md`). Returns up to 5 nearest of each within 8 km, sorted by distance. |
+| National emergency numbers | Curated in `safety-data.js`, cross-checked against `incredibleindia.gov.in/en/emergency` and `india.gov.in/directory/helpline` | Static — these numbers don't change often enough to warrant a live fetch, and a wrong number from an unverified live source would be worse than a curated one. |
+| Seasonal/geographic hazard tags (altitude, cyclone belt, monsoon landslide, seismic zone, desert heat) | Curated in `safety-data.js` | General, well-documented facts about India's geography and seasons (e.g., BIS IS 1893 seismic zoning, Bay of Bengal/Arabian Sea cyclone seasons) used only to decide which *generic* advisory to show — never a claim about current, real-time conditions at a specific moment. |
+
+**Deliberately not included:** per-city fabricated hospital names, phone
+numbers, or state-level helpline numbers. A safety feature that states
+specific facts confidently and gets them wrong is worse than one that
+fetches them live (hospitals/police) or sticks to nationwide numbers that
+are actually verifiable (emergency contacts).
+
+## Safety score
+
+Each destination starts from a **base score** (0-100) determined by its
+category mix (`BASE_SCORE_BY_CATEGORY` in `safety-data.js` — city/heritage
+destinations start higher than remote wilderness ones; this reflects
+tourist-infrastructure density, not a judgment that any destination is
+unsafe in absolute terms). From there, `computeDestinationSafetyScore()`
+applies a signed delta per active hazard factor:
+
+| Factor | Trigger | Typical delta |
+| ------ | ------- | -------------- |
+| Altitude | Destination is in `HIGH_ALTITUDE_IDS` | −8 |
+| Cyclone season | Coastal destination + current month in `CYCLONE_MONTHS` | −10 |
+| Monsoon landslide | Hill-road destination + current month in `MONSOON_MONTHS` | −10 |
+| Seismic zone | Destination is in `HIGH_SEISMIC_ZONE_IDS` | −4 |
+| Extreme heat | Desert destination + current month in `EXTREME_HEAT_MONTHS` | −8 |
+| Wildlife encounter | Destination is in `WILDLIFE_ENCOUNTER_IDS` | −3 |
+| Poor/caution weather | `WeatherCore.computeDaySuitability()` returns `poor`/`caution` | −14 / −6 |
+| Recent nearby earthquake | Magnitude ≥ 4.0 within 300 km in the last 30 days | −10 (≥ 4.0) / −20 (≥ 5.5) |
+
+The final score is clamped to 0-100 and bucketed into four levels:
+**low** (80-100), **moderate** (60-79), **elevated** (35-59), **high**
+(0-34).
+
+## Advisories, checklist & itinerary alerts
+
+- `buildAdvisories()` turns the active factors into a sorted (worst-first)
+  list of human-readable advisory strings.
+- `buildSafetyChecklist()` starts from a fixed base checklist (travel
+  insurance, ID copies, sharing your itinerary, emergency numbers,
+  medication, water safety, night-transport caution, valuables) and adds
+  destination-specific items when a matching hazard factor is active
+  (e.g. an acclimatization-day item when the altitude factor fires).
+  Checked state persists per destination in `localStorage`
+  (`travelSafetyChecklist:<destId>`).
+- `evaluateItinerarySafety()` + `buildItineraryAlerts()` walk a saved Trip
+  Planner itinerary's day-by-day schedule (same shape as
+  `TripPlanner.buildDailySchedule()`) and surface one deduplicated alert
+  per destination that carries at least one non-`info` factor — this is
+  the "alert notifications for itinerary destinations" acceptance
+  criterion. Alerts are date-relative to the day the itinerary is
+  checked, since saved trips don't currently store a start date.
+
+## Known limitations / follow-ups
+
+- Nearby-places and earthquake lookups depend on public third-party APIs
+  (Overpass, USGS) with their own uptime/rate limits; failures are caught
+  per-source so the safety score still renders using whatever succeeded.
+- Hazard tables are a curated heuristic, not a live government-advisory
+  feed — there's no free, no-key, structured API for India-specific
+  travel advisories at the time of writing. A future iteration could
+  integrate one if/when it becomes available.
+- The itinerary-alerts view assumes "today" as the trip start date, since
+  `tripPlannerSavedTrips` doesn't persist a chosen start date. Wiring an
+  actual start date through from Trip Planner (as the dormant
+  `weather-ui.js` feature already expects, via a `#trip-start-date`
+  input) would let both features share the same date context.
